  "markdown": "## TL;DR\n\nI audited the web UI for icon-only controls without accessible names, native `title` use, and inconsistent wording. The real defect was narrower than the task suggested: the global tooltip helper strips a control's `title` on first hover or focus, so any icon-only control named only by `title` lost its accessible name after the first interaction. I gave the four affected components their own `aria-label`, unified one inconsistent tooltip phrase, added a template-wide guard test, and wrote the audit at `docs/qa/2026-11-labels-tooltips-audit-qa.md`. The scoped `REPOOS_CHECK_CHANGED=main repoos check` passed.\n\n## Work and rationale\n\nI started with a grep for `title=\"` and `:title=` across `src/ui-app/src`. That returned about 100 hits, but most are not native bubbles at all. `src/ui-app/src/lib/tooltip.ts` installs one delegated, body-level tooltip that restyles any `title` into a popup. So \"native title\" in this codebase mostly means \"styled tooltip fed by a title attribute\". Replacing all of them would be a large refactor across roughly 40 components, and it is not what this QA pass is for. I left them and recorded the decision in the audit doc.\n\nThe more useful question was whether each icon-only control has a name. I wrote a small Bun scanner in `/tmp` (not committed) that parses `<button>` and `<a>` blocks in every `.vue` file. It flags elements with no visible text, no `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby`, and no dynamic binding. It found nothing, but I checked the scanner against a synthetic icon button first, because a zero result from a regex can just mean the regex is wrong. It fired correctly.\n\nA second scan, for icon-only controls whose only name was a `title`, found eight. Tracing the tooltip code explained why that matters. `enter()` in `tooltip.ts` calls `textFor()`, which removes `title` and stores it in `data-tip`. The accessible name comes from `title` only until the first `mouseover` or `focusin`, after which a keyboard or screen-reader user gets an unnamed button. Three of the eight were `FloatingHeads` head buttons whose `<img alt>` already names them, so they are fine. The remaining five were real:\n\n- `IntegrationStatusBar.vue`: the pipeline collapse toggle.\n- `AgentCard.vue`: the instructions expand/collapse toggle.\n- `AgentModelModal.vue`: two favorite star buttons, one per state.\n- `DiffView.vue`: the previous-file and next-file arrows.\n\nI added `aria-label` to each and kept `title`, so the styled tooltip still shows. For the toggle, the label follows its expanded state, the same way the title does. I considered a central fix in `tooltip.ts` that copies `title` into `aria-label` for controls without visible text. I rejected it for this pass: it changes the name of any control that goes through the helper, and the blast radius needs its own review. It is listed as a follow-up.\n\nFor wording, the drawer's full-screen diff button said \"Open the full-screen diff view\" while `DiffFileViewer.vue` and the other diff controls say \"Open full-screen diff\". I unified the drawer to the shorter form. I checked that no test asserted the old string. I also looked at the removal and dismissal verbs (\"Remove attachment\", \"Delete schedule\", \"Dismiss this error\", \"Dismiss notification\"). Those differences look like they need a product decision on which verb to use, so I documented them rather than changing them.\n\nThe regression test is `src/ui-app/tests/icon-control-names.test.ts`. Its main assertion is a template-wide ratchet: it fails if any button or link under `src/ui-app/src` has no visible text and no name attribute (or non-empty `<img alt>`). It also pins the four new `aria-label` values and the shared diff wording, and includes a fixture case proving the scan flags a title-only button and accepts it once it is named. I wrote the ratchet as a source scan, not a mounted-component test, because mounting `AgentModelModal` and `DiffView` needs store and route setup that would dwarf the check. The source scan catches the class of bug, not just the instances. The first version failed because `import.meta.url` is not a `file:` URL under this runner. Switching to `__dirname`, which the sibling `mobile-touch-affordances.test.ts` uses, fixed it.\n\n## Approaches tried\n\nI first considered a per-component tooltip replacement for every `title`. Rejected as out of scope and risky for a QA pass, since the styled tooltip already works and the real defect is the name loss. I also considered a DOM-level Playwright pass to check computed accessible names after hover. The task allows headless browser checks only for visual verification, and a static guard plus the tooltip-code reasoning covered the same class more cheaply. I did not run a visual check.\n\n## Challenges and blockers\n\nThe first attempt at the guard test used `fileURLToPath(new URL(..., import.meta.url))`. Under the UI vitest config, `import.meta.url` is not a `file:` URL, so the suite failed at import. Using `__dirname` matches the existing tests.\n\nRunning `bunx vitest run` directly failed with `localStorage` undefined in unrelated suites. The fix was to use the repo's UI config (`--config src/ui-app/vite.config.ts`). The affected suites then passed: 87 tests across `favorites`, `agent-model-memory`, `integration-status-bar`, `task-drawer-dirty-diff-button`, `diff-view-escape` and `diff-view-syntax`.\n\nThe scoped check's `tests` step was skipped because the remote validation runner executes the suite (`REPOOS_SKIP_TESTS=1`). I ran the directly affected suites myself, so the full-suite verdict is still left to RepoOS.\n\nNo visual or screen-reader check was run. The change adds attributes and one wording change, so no layout or styling was expected to move. The audit states that a human screen-reader check of the four fixed controls has not been done.\n\n## Key learnings\n\nA title-only name is a latent accessibility bug whenever a helper rewrites `title` at runtime. The tooltip helper was doing its job for sighted users and quietly removing the only name for everyone else. Any control that relies on `title` for its name should also carry `aria-label`, and the guard now enforces that for icon-only controls.\n\nStatic template scans are a cheap, deterministic way to find this class. They are heuristic: they do not evaluate computed names, and a control built from a child component with its own name can slip past. The guard reports on the templates it sees and says so.\n\nNative `title` is not a single problem. In this app it is mostly a styled-tooltip source, so the convention \"no native title tooltip in new UI\" is about new code and new explanations. Rewriting existing ones is a separate refactor, and the audit records it as such.\n\n## Generated change summary\n\nBase tree: `b19e91193927745a7745f2dd557f0e97c658795d`\nSource tree: `b46c5e1fea21a56b65c4cce8fc2571cdb8030de1`\n\n- A docs/qa/2026-11-labels-tooltips-audit-qa.md: +52 / −0\n- M src/ui-app/src/components/AgentCard.vue: +1 / −0\n- M src/ui-app/src/components/AgentModelModal.vue: +2 / −0\n- M src/ui-app/src/components/IntegrationStatusBar.vue: +1 / −0\n- M src/ui-app/src/components/TaskDrawer.vue: +1 / −1\n- M src/ui-app/src/views/DiffView.vue: +2 / −0\n- A src/ui-app/tests/icon-control-names.test.ts: +86 / −0"
